---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---
<Base title="Owner stories" description="What Hyderabad hostel and PG owners found when they moved from register books, WhatsApp groups and Google Sheets to Kamer. Told the way they told us.">
  <section class="page-hero">
    <div class="wrap">
      <span class="eyebrow">Owner stories</span>
      <h1>What the register was hiding</h1>
      <p class="lead">Every owner who moves to Kamer finds something in the first week. Here is what a few of them found, as the founders told it on their podcast. Customer names left out.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section tight">
    <div class="wrap stories">

      <article class="story">
        <div class="story-side">
          <span class="badge">Group of hostels · several partners · Hyderabad</span>
          <div class="found"><span class="hand">Found in week one:</span><br />3 beds, empty 4 months</div>
        </div>
        <div class="story-body">
          <h2>The building the manager said was full</h2>
          <p>A group with four or five hostels and a few partners, run day to day by managers. Nobody at the top walked the floors. Partners judged each building by what the manager reported and by what came in at month end.</p>
          <p>On onboarding day every guest was entered against a bed. In one building the manager had been reporting as full, three beds came up green. They had been empty since the previous quarter. Four months of rent on three beds, gone, and nobody had a reason to doubt the register.</p>
          <p class="pull">Srinivas, who onboarded them, says the partners took control the same day. They now check the bed map every morning. Not because the manager lied, but because until then nobody could see.</p>
          <p class="who">As told by Srinivas Reddy on the founder podcast</p>
        </div>
      </article>

      <article class="story">
        <div class="story-side">
          <span class="badge">Owner-operator · 5 buildings · 500 beds</span>
          <div class="found"><span class="hand">Found in year one:</span><br />advances that shrank</div>
        </div>
        <div class="story-body">
          <h2>Five thousand became three thousand, and nobody knew when</h2>
          <p>This one is ours. Srinivas ran his own buildings on registers for fifteen years. When a guest vacated, the advance came from whatever the book said, and the book had been written by whoever was at the desk that day, six months and a hundred pages earlier.</p>
          <p>A guest who had paid five thousand a year earlier would leave with three or four thousand on record. Some who had paid in full showed as partial. It was never one guest. It was a pattern, and he only saw it once the numbers were somewhere he could look.</p>
          <p>Kamer was the first time the money side of his own hostels was transparent to him. Every advance against a name, every rent entry dated, every settlement in one flow.</p>
          <p class="pull">His line for it is the one on our homepage: wherever he goes now, he carries his hostel. When a travelling guest calls about an advance, he no longer has to promise to check the register when he gets back.</p>
          <p class="who">Srinivas Reddy, co-founder</p>
        </div>
      </article>

      <article class="story">
        <div class="story-side">
          <span class="badge">Every hostel, every month</span>
          <div class="found"><span class="hand">Found on day two:</span><br />the two-day guests</div>
        </div>
        <div class="story-body">
          <h2>A sister for two days, a friend for a weekend</h2>
          <p>Ask any owner about daily bookings and they will laugh. A guest's mother visits for three days. A friend crashes for a weekend. It is a bed, it is occupied, and it is almost never written down, because it is only two days and the manager is busy.</p>
          <p>Srinivas describes the gap plainly. Owners check at the start of the month and at the end. In between there is no close watch, and the two-day guest sometimes stays two or three months. That is two or three months of rent that never came in.</p>
          <p>In Kamer a daily booking is a colour on the bed map. The bed goes pink, the days are counted, the charge is recorded. When the friend is still there in week three, it shows.</p>
          <p class="who">From Srinivas Reddy, on why daily bookings got their own colour</p>
        </div>
      </article>

      <article class="story">
        <div class="story-side">
          <span class="badge">Switched from another app</span>
          <div class="found"><span class="hand">Found by us:</span><br />price wasn't the reason</div>
        </div>
        <div class="story-body">
          <h2>They didn't stay for the price</h2>
          <p>A few owners came to Kamer from a competing app that cost about double. The team assumed price was the whole story, and that they would probably lose those customers to the next cheap option.</p>
          <p class="pull">Siva calls it a pleasant surprise. Those owners stayed because they liked the product. Setup was simple enough to do alone, and their managers could use it without training. That was when the team understood that simplicity was the feature.</p>
          <p>Since then every feature has to pass one test before it ships: can an owner use it without a call from Kamer? If not, it isn't finished.</p>
          <p class="who">Siva Nalavenkata, co-founder</p>
        </div>
      </article>

      <article class="story">
        <div class="story-side">
          <span class="badge">Before the police came</span>
          <div class="found"><span class="hand">Found on inspection day:</span><br />half the IDs missing</div>
        </div>
        <div class="story-body">
          <h2>Half the joining forms had an ID. The rest had nothing.</h2>
          <p>Police verification happens every month in Hyderabad hostels. The joining form is supposed to carry an ID copy. In practice, Srinivas says, only fifty or sixty percent did. Guests are at work from morning to evening, the manager forgot to ask, and by the time an inspection lands it is too late.</p>
          <p>If something happens, the problem lands on the owner, not the guest. He describes the change as two chapters: before Kamer, the forms had a photo and sometimes an ID. After, every guest has KYC, or the app shows exactly who doesn't.</p>
          <p>Aadhaar goes through DigiLocker. Guests can complete their own KYC from Kamer Stay using the hostel code. The owner app shows verified versus pending, so the list is ready before anyone knocks.</p>
          <p class="who">Srinivas Reddy, co-founder</p>
        </div>
      </article>

    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section alt center">
    <div class="wrap narrow center">
      <h2>What did your register hide?</h2>
      <p class="lead">Tell us and we'll add your story here, named or not. WhatsApp <a href="https://wa.me/919100091343" style="color:var(--orange-dark); font-weight:700">+91 91000 91343</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
<style>
  .stories { display: grid; gap: 28px; }
  .story { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 260px 1fr; gap: 32px; background: var(--surface); border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: var(--radius-lg); padding: clamp(24px, 3vw, 40px); box-shadow: var(--shadow); }
  .story h2 { font-size: clamp(1.4rem, 2.6vw, 1.9rem); }
  .found { margin-top: 18px; font-weight: 800; font-size: 1.15rem; line-height: 1.3; }
  .found .hand { font-family: var(--hand); font-weight: 700; font-size: 1.5rem; color: var(--orange-dark); }
  .story-body p { color: var(--ink-2); }
  .pull { font-size: 1.15rem; color: var(--ink); border-left: 3px solid var(--orange); padding-left: 16px; margin: 18px 0 8px; }
  .who { font-size: .9rem; color: var(--muted); margin: 0; }
  @media (max-width: 800px) { .story { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 16px; } }
</style>
